---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/anchored.css";`;

const anatomy = `<!-- The ANCHOR: whatever it is you measure against. Almost always the trigger. -->
<button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger>
  Save
</button>

<!-- The POSITIONER: only placement, never painting, never clicking. -->
<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <!-- The ARROW, optional. Inside, but he places himself against the ANCHOR. -->
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <!-- The CONTENT: what you see. -->
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>
    Saves only every 30 seconds
  </span>
</span>`;

const html = `<div class="sk-anclaje-demo">
  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-start">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      block-start
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>comes out from above</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-end">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      block-end
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>It comes out from below</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="inline-end">
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      inline-end
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Comes out to the side</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor>
    <button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger type="button">
      no arrow
    </button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>The arrow is optional</span>
    </span>
  </span>
</div>`;

const js = `import { initComponents } from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";

initComponents();`;

const escapeHatch = `/* A submenu appears on the side and aligned above: it is not any of the four.
   It is requested by the hook, not by adding a fifth to the public vocabulary. */
.sk-menu__positioner[data-sk-submenu] {
  --sk-anchored-position-area: inline-end span-block-end;
  --sk-anchored-justify: auto;
  --sk-anchored-align: start;
  --sk-anchored-position-try: flip-inline;
}`;

const sameWidth = `/* The listbox measures what its trigger measures. Goes inside the @supports:
   anchor-size() without a resolved anchor is invalid. */
@supports (anchor-name: --a) {
  .sk-select__positioner {
    --sk-anchored-size: anchor-size(width);
  }
}`;

const arrow = `<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content">…</span>
</span>`;

const arrowReact = `<Tooltip arrow content="It keeps itself">
  <button className="sk-button sk-interactive">Save</button>
</Tooltip>

<Popover arrow trigger="Ver perfil" title="Ada Lovelace">
  Mathematician and writer.
</Popover>`;
---

<Base
  title="Anclaje"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="A pattern: placing a box against another element, with the browser as the engine and the machine as the fallback."
>
  <h1>Anchoring</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A box placed against <strong>another</strong> element, not against the page: it leaves one side of the anchor, goes to the opposite side when it does not enter, and disappears if the anchor leaves the screen. This is what tooltip, popover, popup, menu and its submenus, select, combobox and date picker do.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview html={html} js={js} label="HTML" />

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question: <em>could a second component need this exact structure? </em> There is no need to predict anything here, <strong>nine already need it</strong>. So Anchor sends <strong>hooks and</strong> structure, like <a href="/en/vaul">Vaul</a>, and not just variables.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not confused with Vaul, although they both talk about anchoring: a Vaul anchors to a <strong>edge of the viewport</strong>, and this anchors to <strong>another element</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The three parts</h2>
  <p>
    Two flat classes that <strong>make up</strong> with the component parts, such as <code>sk-interactive</code> with <code>sk-button</code>, plus an optional third. They are not a BEM pair because the anchor is not inside the positioner, and in React the positioner is ported to <code>&lt; body&gt; </code>: nesting one under the other would name a containment that does not exist.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={anatomy} label="HTML" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchor</code>, the <strong>anchors</strong>. Whatever it is measured against. Almost always the trigger, never automatically: anchoring is a geometric role and firing is an interactive one. Combobox and date picker anchor the <em>integer</em> control, because the list is aligned to the field being typed, not the 32px chevron next to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored</code>, the <strong>positioner</strong>. It carries the placement and nothing else: it does not paint and it does not intercept the pointer, so the invisible box that it reserves cannot block what is below. Your child, the content, is what is seen and what is clicked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored-arrow</code>, the <strong>arrow</strong>, optional. It is authored or it does not exist. Goes
      inside the positioner, and yet it is placed against the <strong>anchor</strong>, not against the box.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The one that places it is the browser</h2>
  <p>
    Where the <em>CSS Anchor Positioning</em> API is, it places the browser: no layout loop, no measuring on each scroll, with the flipping and missing anchor resolved within the layout engine, which is where that information already lives. Where it is not, position the Zag machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>fallback is not a lower path</strong>, it is the same contract executed by another engine. What it does require is that both do not run at the same time: in the browser path the binding removes from the positioner the inline <code>style</code> that the machine brings, and that is the reason why the pattern declarations can run out of <code>!important</code>. It is structural loading, not verbose: <code>position-try</code> can only flip statements that <strong>not</strong> are <code>!important</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Four placements, on logical axes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>block-start</code>, <code>block-end</code>, <code>inline-start</code> and <code>inline-end</code>, requested with <code>data-sk-placement</code> on the <strong>positioner</strong> and not on the root, because in React the positioner is ported and the inheritance from the root does not reach there. They turn around on their own in RTL.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a <strong>preference, not a</strong> guarantee: if it does not fit, the browser flips it to the opposite side of the same <em>axis as</em>. Whoever asks for <code>inline-end</code> wants the box on the side, and falling on top would be disobeying, not adapting.
  </p>

  <h2>Rare cases use the hook, not a fifth placement</h2>
  <p>
    The vocabulary is not enlarged by exceptions. A submenu wants to appear on the side aligned above, and that is requested by overwriting the hook directly, which is what a hook is for. Letting the exception write the contract is how a game of four becomes a game of nine.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={escapeHatch} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    The other hook that is really used is the width. Select and Combobox measure what your anchor measures:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={sameWidth} label="CSS" />

  <h2>The arrow is optional, and is authored</h2>
  <p>
    A square rotated 45° that peeks over the edge of the box towards the anchor. If the markup does not include <code>.sk-anchored-arrow</code>, there is no arrow, and that is the default. A <a href="/en/components/tooltip">tooltip</a> or a <a href="/en/components/popover">popover</a> want it because they are floating chrome that has to tell <em>what control</em> is talking about; Not a menu or a select, because there the relationship is already stated by the shared border.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrow} label="HTML" />
  <p>In React it is a prop, turned off by default:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrowReact} label="React" />
  <p>
    It is <strong>decorative</strong>, so it always goes with <code>aria-hidden</code>: it does not add
    information, repeats what the placement already gives.
  </p>

  <h3>It comes from the anchor, not from the center of the box</h3>
  <p>
    The difference is seen as soon as the box is moved: <code>anchor-center</code> centers it on the <em>anchor but</em> puts it back on the screen if it does not fit, so a trigger near the edge leaves the box displaced, and an arrow drawn 50% of that box ends up pointing anywhere but at the control. So the arrow is not placed against the box: it is <strong>another box anchored</strong> against the same anchor, with the same <code>position-area</code> and the same flip.
  </p>
  <p>
    And yet <strong>lives inside</strong> of the positioner, which would seem impossible: the spec only allows using as an anchor something that is <em>descendant of the containing block</em> of the element, and the trigger does not hang from the positioner. What solves it is <code>position: fixed</code>: the containing block of a fix is the <strong>viewport</strong>, where the trigger does live, although in the DOM the arrow is still inside. With <code>absolute</code> the containing block would be the positioner and <code>anchor()</code> and <code>anchor-center</code> would be invalid <em>silently</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Staying inside is not prolixity either, it is necessary for three things at the same time. The box of a <a href="/en/components/popover">Popover</a> goes to the <em>top layer</em> when it is opened, and it only enters there with its children. As a positioned daughter, the arrow paints <strong>on top of the</strong> background and edge of the box, which is what makes the seam disappear. And in the <strong>fallback</strong> places the machine, which looks for the arrow inside the floating element.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Flips together with the box</em> because it takes the height of the box as a margin on the outside (<code>anchor-size()</code>): they both have the same footprint, so they cross the threshold of <code>position-try</code> at the same time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's left of the cost: </strong> when flipping, <code>position-try</code> flips margins and alignment but not the <code>rotate</code>, which is not a property it accepts. The rhombus is symmetrical at 180°, so the shape does not change and only which of its sides carry the border change: invisible with the borderless arrow of a tooltip, a line on the side that is not when the border is painted.
  </p>

  <h2>Who uses it</h2>
  <p>
    Seven components make up <code>sk-anchored</code> today. The last column says who resolves the placement: almost everyone stays on the browser route, and the only one that also has machine positioning is the tooltip.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Component</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">What an anchor</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Flecha</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Coloca</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/tooltip">Tooltip</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser + machine (Zag)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/popover">Popover</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/popup">Popup</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes, opt-in</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/menu">Menu</a> and its submenus</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger; the parent item in the submenu</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/select">Select</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the entire control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/combobox">Combobox</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the entire control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/en/components/date-picker">DatePicker</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the entire control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">navegador</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two cuts in the table are the same argument from above, already applied. The arrow is brought by the three who are <strong>floating chrome</strong> and they have to say what control they are talking about; menu, select and combobox do not, because there the border shared with the anchor already says the relationship. And the ones that anchor the <em>integer control</em> are the ones that are written inside: the list is aligned with the field, not with the 32px chevron next to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    It is opt-in, like all patterns: it is imported before the components that consume it, because they are its consumers.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "patterns/anchored.css", probe: "sk-anchored" }]} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* La piel del demo es del consumidor, como siempre: el pattern trae hooks y estructura, no pintura.
     `is:global`: el HTML autorado vive dentro del srcdoc y no lleva el atributo de scope de Astro. */
  .sk-anclaje-demo {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Room above and below so a block-start tooltip has somewhere to go without the browser
       having to flip it: the demo shows the requested placement, not the fallback. */
    padding-block: var(--space-stack-xl);
  }
</style>
